    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nMercedes-Benz Freezes All Investments, Considers Shutting Down Plants\n8 photos\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz\nMercedes-Benz has frozen all new investments at its German facilities and is evaluating potential plant closures. The decision was announced to the staff in an internal memo and escalates a major standoff with labor unions over high domestic manufacturing costs and declining profits.\nChief production officer Michael Schiebe confirmed that the executive board jointly decided to halt all further investments in German sites. Projects that are already underway, such as the billion-euro paint shop at the Sindelfingen plant, will continue as planned, the German media notes.\nSchiebe previously warned that one German assembly plant and one powertran plant could shut down if cost-cutting targets are missed. Furthermore, Human Resources board member Britta Seeger explained that, while the company hopes to avoid closures , it is now forced to examine which German sites it can afford to maintain active and which should be shut down.\nAmid union talks with the works council and IG Metall trade union stalled, the automaker’s management has already pointed out that without changes to domestic cost structures, production of next-generation vehicle models will move exclusively abroad.\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz Management wants workers to increase their standard workweek from 35 to 40 hours per week without a pay raise and accept cuts to special bonuses. In the meantime, the unions are pushing for wage increases, Deutsche Welle reports .\nAccording to internal reports, with results revealed by Michael Schiebe, labor and administrative costs in Germany are up to five times higher than at its plants in Eastern Europe, such as Kecskemet in Hungary, Sebes and Cugir operated through two subsidiaries in Romania, and Jawor in Poland.\nThe potential move mirrors the general turmoil across Germany’s automotive industry, where auto giant Volkswagen, through Porsche and Audi, along with major suppliers, such as Schaeffler, are executing severe restructuring and job cuts.\nFurthermore, demand in China has dropped sharply amid fierce competition from domestic carmakers. China is Mercedes-Benz's largest single-country market, accounting for roughly 30% of its global passenger car sales. While total regional volume in Europe is slightly larger than China alone, China easily outpaces every individual country, including the United States and Germany.\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz Whatever move Mercedes-Benz makes in Germany, it will have no negative impact whatsoever on the automaker’s Tuscaloosa plant in Alabama. In March 2026, Mercedes-Benz Group committed to a $4 billion investment through 2030 to upgrade and expand its Vane facility in Tuscaloosa County, which is part of a broader $7 billion U.S. manufacturing push.\nThe facility will most likely benefit from the brand’s production strategy in Germany. To offset tariffs imposed by the Trump administration, Mercedes is already moving production of its high-volume GLC compact crossover from Germany to the United States.\nAssembly is scheduled to kick off in late 2027, pushing the plant's annual output capacity toward 340,000 vehicles. Mercedes-Benz’s U.S. plant currently employs 5,800 workers and exports about 60% of its output. The plant builds luxury SUVs, such as the GLE , GLS, and all-electric EQE and EQS SUVs.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMercedes-Benz Mercedes-Benz Germany Mercedes-Benz Plant Mercedes-Benz USA Mercedes-Benz plant closure\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT NASCAR Driver Joey Logano Drives His Super Truck for the First Time: It's a Raptor R With a Twist",
